                <pb facs="0921" n="785" xml:id="pg785"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">723.2515/3361: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Peru</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Moore</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Lima</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-05-01T15:00:00-05:00">May 1, 1929—3 p.m.</date><lb/>[Received
                        4:05 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>88. President Leguia and Ambassador Figueroa met this morning and agreed on all
                    points of the proposed treaty except the wording of the clause referring to the
                    boundary line. Chile changed the reading of it but claimed it did not change the
                    sense. President Leguia said he was not altogether convinced and has sent for
                    his engineers. If they decide this clause does not alter the sense of the
                    agreement he will give me a copy of it tonight which I will transmit immediately
                    to you in translation. He will send a copy to his Ambassador in Washington
                    tomorrow for official presentation to you.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Moore</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
